answer_1_baseline:
The Board must take into account several matters when fixing the amount of a monetary penalty:  

- the nature, gravity and duration of the breach;  
- the type and nature of the personal data affected;  
- whether the breach is repetitive;  
- whether the person gained or avoided a loss because of the breach;  
- any mitigation actions taken and their timeliness/effectiveness;  
- whether the penalty is proportionate and effective to secure compliance and deter breach; and  
- the likely impact of the penalty on the person.  

These factors are set out in Sections 33(2)(a)‑(e) and (f)‑(g) of the Act【S1】【S2】.

answer_2_rerank:
The Board must take into account several matters when fixing the monetary penalty, namely:  

* the nature, gravity and duration of the breach;  
* the type and nature of the personal data affected;  
* whether the breach is repetitive;  
* whether the person gained financially or avoided a loss because of the breach; and  
* any steps the person took to mitigate the breach’s effects, including how timely and effective those steps were【S1】.
